WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.040
- Okay.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.830
Hi, good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.890
Thank you so much for
joining us for this talk.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:11.850
My name is Florian Carle,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.980
I'm the manager of the
Yale Quantum Institute,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.490
and the host of this
series of nontechnical talk

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.250
on science and the humanities
that are cosponsored

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.610
by the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.880
This series is trying
to combat science phobia

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.750
and quantum physics phobia too
by introducing other medium

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.349
to the field and talking
about two different topics

00:00:34.349 --> 00:00:36.600
that has the intersection of...

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.857
So in the past,

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:41.220
you can see some of the slides
behind me or on your screens.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.700
In the past, we had talks about
quantum physics and music.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.477
We had physics and dance, we
had mathematics and crochet.

00:00:48.477 --> 00:00:52.451
And today we are very excited
to be joined by Stewart Smith

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:56.790
to talk about "The Hapless Dreamers".

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.785
And so in a very exciting
talk about digital art,

00:01:01.785 --> 00:01:05.640
augmented reality, and quantum simulators.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.400
So we started this series in 2017,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.420
and this is our 14th event of the series.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.530
So to give you a bit of
introduction to Stewart Smith.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.820
So he's a digital artist
and an AR technologist.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.090
He has worked at a very
well renowned hybrid studio

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:29.090
like the Google Creative
Lab and the Data Arts team.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.080
And funded more technology
and strategy consequently.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.702
And is also currently the head of consumer

00:01:37.702 --> 00:01:41.067
augmented reality at
Unity, the gaming giant.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:45.450
And so he received his
MFA from Yale University

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.450
in graphic design, and his
artwork has been exhibited

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.350
in the Museum of Modern Art in New York,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.150
at the ZKM in Germany, at
the SF MOMA in San Francisco,

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.310
and is part of the permanent collection

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.683
at the Fondation Cartier in Paris.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.970
If all of that was not
enough, on the side,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.190
he taught himself how to code,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.930
and he created and maintained

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:18.930
the browser based quantum simulator, QJS.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.940
So he'll talk a bit more about that.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.920
So I think that's something
that's gonna be very exciting

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.300
for the quantum crowd.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.540
So the talk will last about 50 minutes,

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.670
and then you will have time for questions.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.138
For the people on Zoom over there,

00:02:31.138 --> 00:02:34.230
There's a feature at the bottom
of your screen called Q&amp;A,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.760
so feel free to type your question in,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.940
and then we'll take them as they pop in.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.010
Please help me welcome Stewart Smith,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.196
and thank you for being here.

00:02:42.196 --> 00:02:43.701
- Thank you so much.
(audience clapping)

00:02:43.701 --> 00:02:45.240
Hi.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.777
As Florian said, I'm Stewart Smith.

00:02:47.777 --> 00:02:51.123
My background is in art,
code, and graphic design.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.120
But I think it's also important

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.220
to try to find the fun in making things.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.570
I do have a hard time
describing what I mean by that.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.790
And so what I wanna do is step through

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.310
some more visual examples,

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:08.190
and maybe those examples
will promote some discussion,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.029
some arguments, some debate,

00:03:10.029 --> 00:03:13.173
and hopefully just describe
what it is that I do.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.270
I'll give you a little
overview of a few things,

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.790
and then I'll go deep
on about four projects.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.710
So here's a little overview.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.310
I've made music videos
using vintage computers,

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.180
which is a strange thing to
do, but I'd say quite fun.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.430
Games that are played with Windows

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.380
instead of within Windows.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.210
So a version of pong for the browser

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.260
that actually uses popup windows to play.

00:03:39.476 --> 00:03:42.720
Very large panoramic data animations

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:43.920
that take up entire rooms,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.343
so you can walk into this animated data

00:03:46.343 --> 00:03:47.943
that's all around you.

00:03:48.947 --> 00:03:53.010
I've even crafted software synthesizers.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.820
This runs right in any web
browser. It's open source.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.530
You can even plug in a MIDI keyboard

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.900
right into your computer,
and you can play with it.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.095
And with any luck, let's
just see if this...

00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:08.640
(synthesizer playing)

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.920
So there you go, you can try
this out on your own time

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.270
and I'll turn the volume down

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.170
so we don't get a feedback loop there.

00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:18.750
I also got involved with
consumer virtual reality

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.270
when it was just beginning to take off.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.610
So around that time, I
wrote the VR controller

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.910
for a 3D library called Three JS.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.180
It provided an easy generic
way for web-based VR apps

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.240
to support a bunch of
different hand controllers,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.460
'cause you don't wanna
worry about supporting

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.510
a Gear VR controller and Daydream.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.400
You wanna worry about your game, right?

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.170
Or whatever experience you're making.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.620
So this made it a lot easier
to focus on the fun parts,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.297
and I took care of the hard bits for you.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.350
Over at the Google Data Arts team,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.540
we used my VR controller library

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.650
to power this VR music video
for the band LCD Soundsystem.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.978
And I'm gonna talk a little
more about that in a bit.

00:05:01.978 --> 00:05:05.160
You could dance along to the song in VR,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.500
and a recording of your
dance would then become

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.510
part of the video itself.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:14.381
So each one of these cone people
with the little baton hands

00:05:14.381 --> 00:05:16.920
is someone who danced to the song

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.630
and had their dance recorded,

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.530
and then incorporated into this video.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.460
And yeah, you can see some people

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.584
are very enthusiastic about this.

00:05:25.584 --> 00:05:27.870
(audience laughing)

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.910
So I also released "Space Rocks",

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.560
which was just a personal project,

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.030
and not affiliated with Google,

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.090
or really awesome bands or anything.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:37.530
This was my personal tribute

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:42.530
to Atari's 1979 classic,
"Asteroids", but in VR, of course.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.510
And I took my VR controller
library and I added something

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.227
I called multichannel haptic feedback,

00:05:50.227 --> 00:05:54.030
which made it super easy to
add complex haptic commands

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.370
to your VR apps.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.189
So if you need to feel
kickback in the controller

00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:02.010
from firing your laser,
but there's also like

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.430
the hum of the engine revving up and down.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.254
That's really complicated.

00:06:07.254 --> 00:06:10.710
This multichannel haptic
feedback thing made it very easy

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:14.340
to just describe and code
succinctly what you want,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.810
and it takes care of the rest.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.720
So that was a lot of fun, of course.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.000
A few years later,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.480
Oculus upped the game by
adding hand tracking support

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.610
for web based virtual reality.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:30.240
And I immediately wrote
this Handy JS library.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.250
You'll notice JS is a big theme.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.100
I love browser based stuff and JavaScript,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.743
so you'll see that pop up a lot.

00:06:38.628 --> 00:06:40.770
So what this does is it makes recording

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.914
and then recognizing hand poses on the fly

00:06:42.914 --> 00:06:45.360
really quick and easy to do.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.074
So instead of just pinch
gestures to select things,

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:51.767
you can recognize tons of shapes,

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:55.380
including the whole ASL
finger spelling alphabet.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.690
Finger guns is the most fun.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.190
You ever as a child,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.359
just pretend you could shoot
lasers out of your fingers?

00:07:02.359 --> 00:07:05.460
That may sound childish,
but come on, it's fun.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.260
I made it possible, so there you go.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.470
- [Audience Member] You
mean you made it possible

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.733
for us to imagine that.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.060
You only made it possible to-
- That was a bit of a stretch,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.590
yeah, that was a bit of...

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:17.970
I didn't think anyone would call me on it,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.920
but I'm glad you're keeping me honest.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.636
- [Audience Member] But
I work on lasers, if you-

00:07:21.636 --> 00:07:22.658
(Stewart laughing)

00:07:22.658 --> 00:07:24.147
If you could do it with your fingers,

00:07:24.147 --> 00:07:25.739
I'd really like to know how.

00:07:25.739 --> 00:07:26.970
(Stewart laughing)

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.593
So would I. That would be fun.

00:07:29.472 --> 00:07:33.061
So I've made a lot of
work outside VR, too.

00:07:33.061 --> 00:07:35.370
I started coding when I was quite young,

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.820
but I'm educated as a
graphic designer and artist.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.250
I didn't study computer science.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.740
I've worked, well, I
guess you covered this.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.359
I've been a commissioned artist,

00:07:45.359 --> 00:07:47.130
lucky enough to have some work

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.440
in different galleries and museums.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.100
I'm also a lifelong mediocre
to terrible musician,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.190
so you'll see music pop
up a lot in stuff I do.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.650
And I even enjoy writing
essays occasionally,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.723
despite having no lessons to do that.

00:08:01.969 --> 00:08:04.650
I think my friends who are
actually gifted in that medium

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.240
cringe when they read what I've written,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.670
but still, it's fun to write.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.470
It's fun to communicate with people.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.950
Speaking of which, and
cringeworthy things.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.893
I started to become
aware of design as a kid.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.700
I had started this low budget
magazine as a teenager,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.103
or a zine, as the kids say.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.330
Basically, as a ruse, as
a hack, this was a way

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.480
that I could meet and interview
some of my favorite bands,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.050
and their record labels would
send me the music for free.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.480
Pre-digital streaming.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:37.950
But that was pretty amazing.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:39.450
I don't know if you've
ever tried to meet a band

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.270
after a show, they purposely
make it impossible.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.220
But if you get in touch with
the publicist and say, well,

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.570
I don't even really want to meet the band.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.060
I just have this assignment,
I have to interview them.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.393
They're like, oh, well right this way.

00:08:50.393 --> 00:08:52.667
And you get to meet everyone.

00:08:52.667 --> 00:08:55.031
And they will send you all of the ephemera

00:08:55.031 --> 00:08:58.140
and music and albums and
things, and it's great.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:02.687
So I'd do that, and then
I'd also interview musicians

00:09:02.687 --> 00:09:06.682
about the heavy political
topics of the day.

00:09:06.682 --> 00:09:09.090
So that was a way to combine an interest

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.590
in music and politics.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.440
And all of this meant that I,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.210
this graphic design is terrible.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.520
This is teenage graphic design.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.460
But what it exposed me to is
I had to start thinking about

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.958
visual hierarchy and typography,

00:09:22.958 --> 00:09:25.170
the interplay of text and image.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:28.650
So even though this was
terrible, it planted good seeds.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:32.970
Seeds that I would later follow
all the way here to Yale.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.340
So I received my MFA in graphic design,

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.723
just a few blocks over.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.740
Somehow, somehow I squeaked through.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.320
And after Yale, I moved
to Europe for a while.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.930
I became an artist in residence at ZKM,

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.660
or Zakheim, as they'd say.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.150
That's the Center for
Art and Media Technology

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:54.180
in Karlsruhe, Germany,
in Southern Germany.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.060
Beautiful, beautiful place.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.950
Folks there might say
that it's very boring.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.450
I thought it was quiet and lovely,

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.500
and a really good place to get work done.

00:10:03.639 --> 00:10:05.181
But eventually I moved back to the US,

00:10:05.181 --> 00:10:08.159
and joined Google's
Creative Lab in New York,

00:10:08.159 --> 00:10:11.460
which is sort of a subversive
marketing department,

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.380
now that I think about it.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.410
And later I jumped to
Google's Data Arts team

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.153
to do some more music video work.

00:10:19.447 --> 00:10:21.420
I've also spent some time at various

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.520
other large tech companies doing

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.100
whatever large tech companies do.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.350
And I'm currently head of
consumer augmented reality

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.780
at Unity, and everyone knows Unity, right?

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.850
Have you heard? Yeah.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.950
So our game engine is all about making

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:38.070
real time 3D rendering for the masses.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:41.520
70% of the world's games
are made with Unity.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.659
And we're not just 70%
of, sorry, 70% of mobile.

00:10:45.659 --> 00:10:48.030
Definitely make sure I get that right.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:52.110
We're not just 70% of mobile
games, we're 50% of all games.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.490
So if you play games,

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.490
you've played something made with Unity.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.160
So like I said, that's...

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.840
- [Audience Member] Meaning
you're the drive software.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:03.540
So I'm the person who has
no idea what Unity is.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.540
- Unity is a way to make
game development much easier.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:09.690
And distribution.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:13.140
So you could make your game
once in the Unity editor.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.270
And then you can say, I
wanna launch it for Windows,

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.410
I wanna launch it for Mac,

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.530
I wanna put it on VR headsets and whatnot.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.960
And while there's still pain points

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.420
in the development of
complicated software,

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.430
Unity helps grease those wheels,

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.444
and make it much easier to do.

00:11:28.444 --> 00:11:30.180
- [Audience Member] Does it have its own

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.790
programming language or
just use existing ones?

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.740
- Unity is primarily C# focused.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:42.740
So if you're familiar
with C# or Java, C, C++,

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:47.043
any of those things will
translate nicely into Unity stuff.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.880
Happy to talk more about
Unity too, after all this,

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.939
if you wanna talk about that.

00:11:52.939 --> 00:11:56.250
But I wanna show you
four things that I think

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.630
are kind of different from each other,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:02.163
and it'll give you a sense of
how I think about this stuff.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:06.417
So the first one is a
project called "Racer".

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.153
This came out of Google Creative Lab.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.150
Google's Chrome team was enjoying

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.820
this big market share for desktop.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.580
So Google was very happy.

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.610
However, Chrome on mobile
was not so popular.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.640
I think just people
weren't really aware of it.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.583
And so the Google Chrome team asked us

00:12:27.583 --> 00:12:30.431
to make an ad campaign.

00:12:30.431 --> 00:12:32.730
Typical marketing ask.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:36.630
Like let's do maybe some TV
spots, posters in the subway,

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:37.950
stuff like that.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.500
And we said, no. (laughs)

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.822
We said, we have a better idea.

00:12:42.822 --> 00:12:46.410
We were starting to
obsess over racing games,

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:49.023
and slot car racing games in particular.

00:12:50.257 --> 00:12:55.257
And I started brainstorming
just random ideas about tracks

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.280
made of software that
could change on the fly,

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:00.333
that were modular.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.760
And I also created some quirky crash demos

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.310
that were inspired by early vector games

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.953
like "Asteroids" and
"Tempest" and whatnot.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.680
And we also had this idea
about racing across screens.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:20.040
So you literally needed to have
multiple phones or tablets,

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.083
or what have, you next to each other.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.320
It was taking this idea of social gaming,

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:27.810
and pushing you physically together.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:29.493
This was obviously pre pandemic.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.620
It'd be a little strange
to push that today.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:36.510
And so this was the final result.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.792
We said, no posters in the subway.

00:13:39.792 --> 00:13:41.940
No TV spots.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.730
Let's make this game that
shows off how powerful

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.710
Chrome for mobile really is.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.245
So you don't just say, we can show you.

00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:50.610
And we can make it fun.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.263
So you actually want to be shown.

00:13:53.562 --> 00:13:56.310
Our racing game needed a soundtrack.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.950
And we actually convinced
this guy Giorgio Moroder

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:00.720
to score the soundtrack.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.510
And he wrote individual
tracks for each phone.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:06.180
So one phone would be
playing the percussion,

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:09.240
one would have like the
lead melody and so on.

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:12.148
And if you're not familiar with Giorgio,

00:14:12.148 --> 00:14:14.850
he's an electronic disco legend.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:19.083
And he's collaborated with
Daft Punk more recently.

00:14:20.122 --> 00:14:23.220
He's a legend in the
community, I can't believe,

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.220
I still can't believe
we got him to do this.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.070
So those were all the
ingredients for this game,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.351
and here's what that
experience looked like.

00:14:32.351 --> 00:14:36.068
(cars revving)
(thumping electronic music)

00:14:36.068 --> 00:14:38.818
(text whooshing)

00:14:41.329 --> 00:14:44.752
(phone tapping)

00:14:44.752 --> 00:14:47.419
(phone beeping)

00:14:49.705 --> 00:14:52.455
(phones beeping)

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:55.550
(phones beeping)

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.133
(cars revving)

00:15:00.631 --> 00:15:03.214
(cars revving)

00:15:04.824 --> 00:15:07.407
(cars revving)

00:15:10.809 --> 00:15:13.559
(phones beeping)

00:15:31.833 --> 00:15:35.550
So I think you can see how
you need to hug the track.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:37.860
I should have maybe explained beforehand,

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:40.110
if you're not familiar with slot cars,

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.060
the whole idea is that
you don't get to steer.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.670
You just have one big dumb gas pedal.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.830
And you need to go as
fast as you possibly can

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:47.940
on the straightaways,

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.620
and then let up when you hit the curves,

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:50.970
otherwise you spin off the track.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.760
And so we replicated that behavior here,

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:58.740
where your beautiful
flagship multi-touch device

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.260
was just used as one
big dumb button, which,

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.140
we thought there was some humor in that.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:06.024
(Stewart laughing)

00:16:06.024 --> 00:16:10.080
So, Sundar Pichai, who
is now Google's CEO,

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:12.780
He unveiled Racer at Google IO that year,

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:14.820
and the tech press loved it.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.430
And so that's kind of
what we were aiming for,

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.260
was this recognition
in the tech community,

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:20.910
and then also in the broader community,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.100
people just playing the game for fun.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.160
But we also struck a chord
with the music press as well,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.460
which we didn't really
expect, and that was awesome.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.200
People were really getting onto the fact

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:34.800
that Giorgio Moroder had,
aside from Daft Punk,

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.623
he had come out of
retirement for us to do this.

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:43.050
Off the record, we made a
limited vinyl run pressing

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:47.643
of the soundtrack, and Giorgio
even signed our copies.

00:16:48.538 --> 00:16:49.923
That was pretty great.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.970
This was a fun project on
its own, but as a nice bonus,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.280
we were awarded the Cannes
Lion Gold for creativity

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.620
and innovation on mobile devices.

00:16:58.620 --> 00:16:59.910
You probably don't know what that is,

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.480
'cause it's a silly industry
award for like marketing.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.978
But it just means people
thought it was cool.

00:17:05.978 --> 00:17:07.606
(Stewart laughing)

00:17:07.606 --> 00:17:09.330
That made us happy too.

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.520
So sadly, Racer is no longer live,

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.230
I think some of the website still exists,

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.029
but it'll say something like,
your network is too slow.

00:17:16.029 --> 00:17:18.450
Which is just a fake error code for,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:20.700
we were only gonna support this
for about 12 months anyway,

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:22.023
as a marketing campaign.

00:17:22.877 --> 00:17:24.570
And now it's off the radar.

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:25.710
Otherwise I'd race you all,

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:27.543
we could have a competition up here.

00:17:28.599 --> 00:17:31.260
(indistinct)

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.760
All right.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.897
Next project, Autocompose.

00:17:35.879 --> 00:17:38.310
This is a much less visual project,

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.740
and I think it demonstrates a predilection

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.560
for combining interests in
product design, marketing,

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:46.290
and also cutting edge research,

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.440
just kind of blending all
of these interests together.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:54.440
So back in 2013 I had more
or less accidentally followed

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:59.040
ex-Google star Marissa Mayer to Yahoo.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.410
And the narrative there was
that Marissa would rescue this

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:05.550
once great company that at
this point was falling apart.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:09.333
And Yahoo Mail in particular
was in need of rescuing.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.940
So we got this official ask
from the Yahoo Mail team,

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:16.410
from the product team.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:20.253
They wanted a Valentine's
themed marketing campaign.

00:18:21.918 --> 00:18:24.360
And I guess that just came to mind,

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.070
'cause it was late
January and they're like,

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.920
what's a reason to promote something?

00:18:29.331 --> 00:18:32.010
Regardless of the reasoning,
they gave us this ask

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.680
near the end of January,
which meant we had about

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.650
three weeks until Valentine's Day hit.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.260
And also because we needed to
put all these plans in motion

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.900
and into production
ahead of Valentine's Day,

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:44.850
we really had about two weeks.

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:49.320
So, it's kind of a nightmare scenario when

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:51.720
someone comes to you and
says, two weeks from now,

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.210
we need to have something
that changes the narrative

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:56.850
around Yahoo Mail from
everyone being upset

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:59.714
to people saying, oh,
isn't that a nice product?

00:18:59.714 --> 00:19:02.220
(Stewart laughing)

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:04.953
So I had an idea for this.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.700
It's an idea that I'd played
with a few years prior

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:11.400
during the height of the iSomething craze.

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:14.610
So think iMac, iTunes, iLife,

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:18.206
and the crown jewel of the time, the iPod.

00:19:18.206 --> 00:19:23.206
iPhone and iPad were still
super secret skunkworks projects

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.203
that no one knew about.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:30.180
And that idea was, iQuit.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.940
I apologize for the language.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.090
This was a resignation letter generator.

00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:38.400
So your job, your
relationship, your friends,

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:41.370
get out of anything instantly with iQuit.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.100
This is a real...
(audience member laughing)

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:45.120
Useful, right?

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.030
This is a real functioning
website that I launched.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:51.330
You put in the email address
of who you want to quit,

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:52.860
and it would do the rest.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:56.430
It would generate a random
formal letter of resignation,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:57.453
and send it to them.

00:19:58.454 --> 00:20:01.230
Yes, very useful.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:04.203
The faux testimonials I had
on the website were absurd.

00:20:05.977 --> 00:20:08.970
"I used to do corporate
advertising until I realized

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.110
having a soul is nice.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:13.785
I fired up iQuit and bam,
now I can starve with pride."

00:20:13.785 --> 00:20:15.090
(audience laughing)

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:19.200
It's just kind of ridiculous
faux testimonials.

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.153
Here's an actual sample email

00:20:21.153 --> 00:20:24.003
that it generated
automatically on the fly.

00:20:25.357 --> 00:20:28.560
"This email serves as my
formal letter of resignation,

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.210
effective immediately.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.550
It's not my intention to injure
feelings with this letter,

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.530
but to merely illuminate a path

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.410
toward a more functional
environment for myself."

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:41.370
And it goes, it's ridiculous, right?

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:43.074
- [Audience Member]
Did you write all this?

00:20:43.074 --> 00:20:45.690
- The way that I made
this original version is,

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.720
I had a list of opening lines,

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:53.720
tons of middling lines, and
then a few choice closers,

00:20:54.802 --> 00:20:57.270
and it would randomly
assemble those on the fly.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:00.193
- [Audience Member] So they
are kind of randomly assorted.

00:21:02.252 --> 00:21:04.110
- And I'm glad you asked about that,

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:07.593
because that's the seeds of
what we're about to talk about.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:12.123
So that's when it hit me,
this Valentine's Day campaign.

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:15.453
Writing love letters is hard.

00:21:17.205 --> 00:21:21.627
"Dear Jane, you are so neat."

00:21:21.627 --> 00:21:25.080
(audience laughing)

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.250
It's a pain, right?

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:28.800
So I quickly, taking that sort of humor,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:32.010
I coded this demo that
constructed love letters

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:35.850
automatically right in the
Yahoo Mail compose window.

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:36.990
And if you didn't like the result,

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:38.490
you could just edit it yourself,

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:40.800
or click the auto compose button again

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.123
for a new composition.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.160
So you can see it just
cycling through, you click,

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.490
and then it gives you these things.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:53.336
I also created this interface
for our copywriters,

00:21:53.336 --> 00:21:56.460
so they could craft their
lines and test them.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:57.293
This is really,

00:21:57.293 --> 00:22:00.720
this is like a showcase for
a good copywriter, right?

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:03.270
Because it's all about the
language that goes in there.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.860
It's really all about the text.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.271
And I feel like you rarely get to do that

00:22:07.271 --> 00:22:10.281
in a tech marketing medium.

00:22:10.281 --> 00:22:12.000
But here it is.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.390
So I wrote the
pseudo-programming language.

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:18.060
So literally they can just write opener,

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:19.560
have a whole list of openers.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.824
And then other, there's like
"Mad Libs" style replacements

00:22:22.824 --> 00:22:25.290
and things that it could do as well.

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:28.680
And it would automatically
take what you've done in here

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:33.680
and use it as the contents
for what was being generated.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.030
And this way they could test

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:37.740
if their lines really worked together.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.720
Sometimes a brilliant line of text

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:41.790
seems like it could flow into another,

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:43.620
and then you see them juxtaposed,

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:46.470
and the language doesn't feel right.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:48.510
So this was a way that
they could gut check

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:50.790
a bunch of compositions.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:52.623
And also it was really important to me

00:22:52.623 --> 00:22:55.170
that the number of permutations possible

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:59.460
exceeded the number of daily active users.

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:03.675
So that in theory, every
single person using Yahoo Mail

00:23:03.675 --> 00:23:05.391
could generate a love letter,

00:23:05.391 --> 00:23:07.413
and every one of them would be unique.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.040
So it was a lot.

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:14.280
And we launched it in
time for Valentine's Day,

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.577
and it was a success.

00:23:16.577 --> 00:23:19.620
We were able to change the
conversation around Yahoo Mail,

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:24.620
from hate at the time, to
something a bit more amused.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:29.373
People were at least
smiling and sharing this.

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.073
Yeah, the sentiment was
positive for a while.

00:23:37.127 --> 00:23:39.570
So because auto compose was a hit,

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.850
we immediately started looking for ways

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.860
to take it even further.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.630
And you had asked about
how it was constructed,

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:47.220
and it was just lines of text,

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:49.503
and they were randomly assembled together.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.950
That was good for a two week project,

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:56.760
but to really go further, we
needed actual machine learning.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:58.920
And thankfully, right around that time,

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:02.010
Yahoo had purchased this
startup called Summly.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:04.328
And what it did was
automatically summarize

00:24:04.328 --> 00:24:07.653
long news articles into
very short snippets.

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:13.060
So I was able to take these ideas together

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.940
and sketch that out into a
patent for using existing emails

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:22.320
as training data for
constructing new email drafts.

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:27.000
So you have an email corpus
of all of your missives

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:31.230
to friends, your mom, or whatever,

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.360
and the way that you interact
with different people

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:34.590
will be different.

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:36.180
If you're emailing a professor,

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.250
I would imagine you don't
speak in the same way

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:40.440
that you might speak with
some of your friends.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:43.440
There may be a bit of more
formality or what have you.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:44.273
Or maybe not. I don't know.

00:24:44.273 --> 00:24:46.509
I don't know your professors individually.

00:24:46.509 --> 00:24:47.940
(laughs)

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:51.360
But the point was that we
could use machine learning

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:54.810
to look at those
correspondences and then predict

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:58.020
or imagine what another
correspondence would be

00:24:58.020 --> 00:24:59.910
between you and that recipient.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:03.210
So let's say you have the flu,

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:05.280
and you need to find a way
to apologize to your buddies

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:07.350
for missing an important birthday.

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:08.940
If you're really feeling sick,

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.170
do you wanna write that email,

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:13.473
or just let the computer write
it for you and apologize?

00:25:14.757 --> 00:25:18.456
I'm skeptical about whether
we want any of this stuff.

00:25:18.456 --> 00:25:21.074
But it was fun to work on.

00:25:21.074 --> 00:25:22.050
- [Audience Member] Well you're certainly

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:23.522
making everybody lazier.

00:25:23.522 --> 00:25:26.848
(Stewart and audience laughing)

00:25:26.848 --> 00:25:29.970
A few years later, Google
did the same thing.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:33.000
And instead of auto compose,
they called it Smart Reply.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:34.980
So I'm not saying I invented Smart Reply,

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:37.710
but maybe it was just
in the air at the time.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:39.000
I like to float that and pretend

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:40.450
I'm more important than I am.

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:43.917
Anyway, I wrote about all of this

00:25:43.917 --> 00:25:46.050
in an essay called "The Charm Offensive".

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:48.180
So if you're curious to know more,

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.300
a semi humorous essay
about how this came to be.

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:54.573
Okay, sorry, second to last
one here, called Dance Tonite.

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:59.190
So virtual reality has
become a big part of my life

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.260
for the past few years.

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:03.210
And the first VR system that I ever tried

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.460
was a Vive dev kit.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.070
And once I tried it, I knew immediately

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:09.843
I wanted to make stuff for VR.

00:26:11.381 --> 00:26:15.609
But I'm a web guy, so I
had to find other people

00:26:15.609 --> 00:26:18.630
who were building web-based VR tools.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.967
And this was in the very
early days of this stuff,

00:26:20.967 --> 00:26:23.190
and I started making
little demos like this,

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:26.129
where you'd be in this
weird rippling environment

00:26:26.129 --> 00:26:28.950
of this kind of dream sand substance

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:30.903
that would swirl together to make objects.

00:26:30.903 --> 00:26:33.660
I don't know, I thought it
was very artistic at the time.

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:36.750
And now I'm like, well, it's
a decent tech demo, I guess.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:38.853
But these were the early days.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.360
I also built some early co-presence demos.

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.540
And again, all this stuff
was running in the browser.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:49.020
One day I got about 80
folks in Google Creative Lab

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:51.360
to join me in this little VR universe.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:54.300
So everyone was these sort of peg people,

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:55.980
and they could look around and everyone

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:58.470
would have this randomly generated name

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.140
that was a random color
and a random animal.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:04.069
And you could run around
like little Mario characters

00:27:04.069 --> 00:27:07.110
and teleport between worlds,
but that was about it.

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:09.240
That was about the extent
of the interaction.

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:13.154
But for late 2015, not bad.

00:27:13.154 --> 00:27:15.033
That was a technical feat.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:20.250
I created this day and
night web app for my kids.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:21.570
Their grandparents live in Europe,

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:23.310
and I wanted to give them
a better understanding

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:25.050
of how time zones work.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.930
So here, they can float in space.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.780
You put the headset on
and you're in outer space

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.940
above this rotating Earth.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:35.700
And the city lights
come on as night falls,

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.940
and you can get a sense of how
it is the middle of the day

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.178
in one spot in the middle
of the night in another,

00:27:41.178 --> 00:27:44.851
in a very visual, visceral sense.

00:27:44.851 --> 00:27:48.450
So this universe is movable and scalable.

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.160
I was making something for my kids,

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.950
who are obviously a
little shorter than I am.

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:55.867
So the whole idea was that
you could grab the world

00:27:55.867 --> 00:27:58.140
as if you were going to do pinch and zoom,

00:27:58.140 --> 00:27:59.430
like on your phone.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:01.860
And you could expand the
universe or contract it,

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:04.950
and rotate it, and put it
wherever was comfortable.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.410
So you'd have the earth
kind of about this size,

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:08.730
just floating here.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:09.660
And that's fun.

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.628
Or you could make it huge, and
you could be kind of floating

00:28:12.628 --> 00:28:14.700
right next to the curvature of the Earth

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:17.100
and watching as it shifts
between day and night.

00:28:20.007 --> 00:28:23.196
This was another thing
that I was working on

00:28:23.196 --> 00:28:26.710
that does not seem to flow so smoothly

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.310
when we're trying to mirror screens here.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:34.310
But this was a giant data
visualization of flight patterns.

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:37.890
And I recorded this to VR, and
you could do the same thing.

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:39.630
You could expand it and
you could actually sit

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:43.320
inside the Earth and look
out and see, well, here,

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:46.620
if we're not over the ocean,
there's a bit more to look at.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:48.570
You could actually see all of this stuff

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:52.953
swirling around you, which
was viscerally a lot of fun.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:57.770
And so I was working on this VR stuff

00:28:57.770 --> 00:28:59.640
while I was at Creative Lab.

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:01.800
And then I moved over
to the Data Arts team,

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.780
which is another team within Google.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:04.710
And this was the first thing

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:06.310
that I put in motion over there.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.320
I won't dwell too much on it
since I mentioned it earlier,

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.150
but it was just a way to make

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:14.403
working with VR controllers easier.

00:29:16.659 --> 00:29:19.020
But I'm happy to dwell
again on "Asteroids",

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:20.160
because I love that game.

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:24.720
So here it is translated into VR.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.150
Or if we go back for a moment.

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:32.150
The original "Asteroids" is a
black and white vector game.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:35.280
And that's because of the hardware.

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:37.410
It was built to run on a Vectrex screen.

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:40.380
So speaking of lasers, well I guess,

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:41.741
would you consider that a real laser?

00:29:41.741 --> 00:29:42.885
- [Audience Member] No.

00:29:42.885 --> 00:29:43.953
- No, okay.

00:29:43.953 --> 00:29:45.463
(laughs)

00:29:45.463 --> 00:29:46.920
I like to think of it as a laser,

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:49.530
but I realize that's just
because it sounds cooler,

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:51.750
but it's not a real, yeah.

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.300
Anyway, photon gun, electron gun?

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:55.350
That's more?
- Yeah.

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:56.910
Electron gun is different.

00:29:56.910 --> 00:29:57.743
- Yeah.

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:01.020
So that's why it looks this way.

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:02.820
And I wanted to translate it to VR.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.820
And at first I was
tempted to make something

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:07.200
that looked like this,

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.440
but I realized conceptually
I should probably be doing

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:11.850
what the hardware wants,

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:15.027
and in a way that is truer
to the original game.

00:30:15.027 --> 00:30:19.553
And so that's why it ends
up being these flat surfaces

00:30:19.553 --> 00:30:22.662
with shading to indicate their depth

00:30:22.662 --> 00:30:25.203
and where they are in space.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:32.610
So with all of those little
VR pieces in my palette,

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:36.930
and now with the Data Arts
team, that's when I made,

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.590
and when I say I, I mean we,
the team, made "Dance Tonite".

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:43.050
And it's a ever changing
dance collaboration

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:47.193
between LCD Soundsystem and
their fans in virtual reality.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:53.043
So when you load it up, you
get this little tutorial.

00:30:54.284 --> 00:30:56.700
This ball represents the camera,

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.530
it enters the room and the room lights up,

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:00.450
and that means it's time to dance.

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:05.370
And each room represents
about eight seconds of music.

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.350
That's the amount of time it takes

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:10.351
for the phrasing of the
music to turn around.

00:31:10.351 --> 00:31:13.380
So you record your dance.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:16.710
And then you can actually
record multiple dances.

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:19.230
So it could just be you doing this,

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:20.700
but you could build up, I think, yeah,

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:22.710
20 copies of yourself if you want to.

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:27.056
So you can start to make more
complex movements and things,

00:31:27.056 --> 00:31:29.103
even if you're just on your lonesome.

00:31:30.544 --> 00:31:32.610
Here's what it looks like from the inside,

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:33.600
which is a little chaotic,

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.673
because this is someone actually dancing.

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:40.073
And...

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:45.230
Let's see if I can do this
with sound without feedback.

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:50.153
("Tonite" by LCD Soundsystem)

00:31:55.015 --> 00:31:56.880
You can see the balls...

00:31:56.880 --> 00:31:59.797
(feedback echoing)

00:32:01.698 --> 00:32:04.433
♪ Everybody's singing the same song ♪

00:32:04.433 --> 00:32:06.562
Okay, I will turn down the sound,

00:32:06.562 --> 00:32:08.820
so I don't get that feedback.

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:10.470
So this way I can talk
through something like,

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:12.840
each one of these people is selectable.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:16.890
So let's say, I'll grab one
of these folks over here.

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:17.820
And when I click on them,

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:19.440
this is their point of view camera.

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:23.040
So you can relive that dance experience,

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.730
which may make you sick,
since it's this added motion.

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:29.880
You can also, let's see where the ball is.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.620
You can also grab the ball.

00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:34.140
And this is a little more
of a steady cam experience.

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:36.510
So you are now the ball
floating through here.

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:38.010
And if you do this in a VR headset,

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:39.510
you don't just have the
straight ahead view.

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:40.710
You can just look around,

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:42.900
and it's like you're on
this slow moving carriage.

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:45.813
And you can see what's
happening everywhere else.

00:32:46.864 --> 00:32:50.133
Let's see if I can jump
on the timeline here.

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:53.490
It goes on to this other
part of the universe,

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:57.221
where you're no longer
constrained to these rooms.

00:32:57.221 --> 00:32:59.940
And you can see all
these other folks dancing

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.950
and reacting to the music.

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:06.693
And I think you get the
general idea for this video.

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:13.230
So we launched it at Google IO, again,

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:15.810
and we had this big party.

00:33:15.810 --> 00:33:18.000
You can see all of this ridiculous,

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:19.290
we actually built rooms,

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:22.320
physical rooms that correspond
to those virtual rooms.

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.220
So you have this trippy experience

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:28.320
where you step into the physical
room, put on the headset,

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:29.910
you're in the virtual room.

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:31.980
And also people are in a ball pit,

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:33.480
which seems like it should not be real,

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:34.743
but that was physical.

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:37.380
Kids as adults.

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:39.030
You could write books about that.

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:45.333
And here's, yeah, just a
bit more of am overview.

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.180
I'm not making fun of this
guy by having him here.

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:50.190
He's actually a very well known,

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:51.630
well-respected VR journalist,

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:53.816
and I'm so excited-
- I thought that was Delorean.

00:33:53.816 --> 00:33:54.917
- [Stewart] No.

00:33:54.917 --> 00:33:56.146
(Stewart and audience laughing)

00:33:56.146 --> 00:34:00.870
- [Audience Member] I'll take
it, can I do it in the room?

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:02.520
- So that was a lot of fun,

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:04.743
and a fun way to launch
the project as well.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:09.203
All right.

00:34:09.203 --> 00:34:11.913
So this is my fourth and
final project to show.

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:14.490
Since this is for YQI,

00:34:14.490 --> 00:34:16.650
I should probably talk about something

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:18.363
quantum related maybe.

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:22.083
But before I do, I wanna
make something clear first.

00:34:23.430 --> 00:34:26.520
Let's say that this circle
represents quantum physics.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.680
This is all the knowledge
about how the universe

00:34:28.680 --> 00:34:30.360
actually conducts itself, right?

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:33.660
Photons, locality,
thermodynamics, packets of light,

00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:36.399
whatever, whatever you want to talk about.

00:34:36.399 --> 00:34:38.490
And over here is quantum computing.

00:34:38.490 --> 00:34:40.980
And this is pure math and logic,

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:44.640
and you don't need to know
so much about electrons

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:48.333
or superconducting to just
write computer programs.

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:51.090
And of course you have that intersection,

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:54.093
the actual quantum hardware,
quantum computers themselves.

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:58.140
But this is the realm
that I'm more comfortable

00:34:58.140 --> 00:35:00.300
speaking about because
I am not a physicist,

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.040
I'm not an engineer.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:03.960
And if we're being totally honest,

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:07.260
my personal knowledge scape
looks a little more like this.

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:09.570
Random puddles of knowledge.

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.970
Even this is generous, by the way.

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:13.830
rather than a vast ocean of knowledge

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:15.630
that I think many of you folks have.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:20.610
But also this, 'cause we
humans are beautifully messy,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:22.680
nonsensical creatures.

00:35:22.680 --> 00:35:25.290
So with this disclaimer in mind,

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.540
I can talk about some
of my quantum projects.

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:30.360
So I'm the creator and maintainer

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:33.213
of Quantum JavaScript, or QJS for short.

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:37.050
And I'm the founder of Open Quantum Club.

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:39.390
So what is this stuff exactly?

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:41.400
We can go deep on QJS for a second.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.233
It's actually a few things at once.

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:46.350
It's this website,

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:49.650
and the corresponding
source code repository.

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:52.380
It's this drag and drop
circuit composer interface,

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:53.403
in particular.

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:56.130
It's a quantum circuit simulator,

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:57.960
because this is just software, right?

00:35:57.960 --> 00:35:59.973
It's not actual quantum hardware.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.473
It's the entire code library
and its corresponding API.

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:08.280
It's some concept primers that are there

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:10.260
to help get folks like me up to speed

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:12.270
on quantum computing quickly.

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.760
You know, folks with no
real physics background

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.730
or quantum background, just casual coders

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:19.533
with some rusty high school math.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:21.690
It turns out that's all you need

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:24.060
to start making some fun mistakes.

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:25.960
And then you can learn more as you go.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:30.990
And it's the documentation
too, for the QJS API,

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:32.430
because what good is a library

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:34.260
if you don't know how to use it?

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:36.330
And when you put all of this together,

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:39.390
there's a small, but growing
community of enthusiastic folks

00:36:39.390 --> 00:36:42.159
who have also fallen in love
with this ridiculous idea

00:36:42.159 --> 00:36:45.720
of fusing quantum and web browsers.

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:48.270
Because JavaScript wasn't
chaotic enough on its own, right?

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:49.920
Do we have any JavaScript coders here?

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:51.543
Know what I'm talking about? No?

00:36:52.953 --> 00:36:54.930
(sighs)

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:57.423
Really nerdy jokes. I'm so sorry.

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:00.210
So for this community,

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:02.227
I'm starting something called the OQC,

00:37:04.896 --> 00:37:06.960
the Open Quantum Club.

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:10.391
And this is a project, I'm
just spinning it up right now,

00:37:10.391 --> 00:37:13.320
through a website that will
feature profiles and interviews

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:15.420
with folks in the quantum community.

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:17.640
So the special focus here
will be on the humanity

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:21.183
behind the math, since that's
what I'm better at, obviously.

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:24.570
So who are the personalities?

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:26.250
What are the collective hopes and dreams

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:27.600
and that sort of thing?

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:31.443
So stay tuned for more about
that as we head towards 2023.

00:37:33.275 --> 00:37:35.260
But let's talk about the, oh yeah?

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:37.863
- [Audience Member] Is it more
like an interview project?

00:37:37.863 --> 00:37:40.142
- Yes, yeah, and I've
conducted my first interview

00:37:40.142 --> 00:37:42.810
and forgot how difficult transcribing

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.040
a long interview could be.

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:45.447
So I need to finish that.

00:37:45.447 --> 00:37:48.360
And the website is partially finished.

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:51.660
So it's in progress,
but not ready to launch,

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:53.883
but yeah, mostly interview based.

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:57.600
So this is the QJS website,

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:01.050
which is really the hub for
the whole QJS experience.

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:03.720
There are quantum tutorials,
the API documentation,

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:05.460
all the things I just mentioned.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.328
I'm gonna play a quick demo video for you

00:38:07.328 --> 00:38:10.380
that I made to show off
some of the features.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:12.630
Okay, all right, Quantum JavaScript,

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:16.062
it starts off with this interactive

00:38:16.062 --> 00:38:18.150
circuit building interface that runs

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:19.950
right in your web browser.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:22.043
And I'll explain what these
things are in a moment,

00:38:22.043 --> 00:38:24.510
but I'll just show you
how it operates first.

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:28.890
We can select everything and
throw it away and start over.

00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:31.770
We also have an undo button
here, which is great.

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:35.520
And also a lock button, so
we can lock our circuit,

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.220
and can't mess it up.

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:40.716
Can't select things,
can't throw stuff away,

00:38:40.716 --> 00:38:44.104
which is even more helpful
on a mobile interface,

00:38:44.104 --> 00:38:47.147
where there is no hover
state and selection,

00:38:47.147 --> 00:38:49.064
you're tapping.

00:38:49.064 --> 00:38:50.940
Perhaps you're tapping to scroll,

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:53.303
perhaps you're tapping without meaning to,

00:38:53.303 --> 00:38:55.849
and you definitely don't
want to mess up a circuit

00:38:55.849 --> 00:38:57.783
that you've carefully composed.

00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:02.180
But in this case, we can
throw down an identity gate

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:05.670
and maybe a Poly X gate and select them

00:39:05.670 --> 00:39:10.670
and combine them together to
make a controlled gate here.

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:15.890
And what we've just created
here is a bell state.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.921
And even if you don't
understand any of this stuff,

00:39:18.921 --> 00:39:23.225
you can see the live probability results

00:39:23.225 --> 00:39:27.423
being updated in real
time as we change things.

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:30.150
So it's really cool

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:32.850
that you can drag and drop stuff together,

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:37.850
but you can also code things,
code circuits with JavaScript.

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:42.000
And what's even more than that
is you can combine the two.

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:46.050
So if we open up our
JavaScript console here.

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:47.823
Let me clear it out.

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:53.460
It says that this circuit is
accessible in our console.

00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:56.040
And so let's give that a shot, let's see.

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:57.420
We get a circuit back.

00:39:57.420 --> 00:39:59.320
Here, let's make this a little larger.

00:40:00.870 --> 00:40:03.840
Let's see what that circuit looks like.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:05.070
We can get a diagram of it.

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:08.613
It's not very interesting
when we have a blank circuit.

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:14.640
But here, we can quickly
build that bell state again.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:17.460
And you can see as I'm
editing the circuit,

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:21.993
we're getting real output, just like that.

00:40:23.103 --> 00:40:24.450
And here, let me scroll up,

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:28.500
so we can actually see
that this ASCII diagram

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:31.683
looks very similar to what we
have right here, doesn't it?

00:40:33.324 --> 00:40:35.310
And so that is pretty cool.

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:40.052
And what's even cooler is we
can start to do things like,

00:40:40.052 --> 00:40:44.910
let's say we want to add
another one of those H gates.

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:45.990
That's a Hadamard gate,

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:48.360
and we'll talk about that in a second.

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:53.360
Let's add it to moment
three, on wire three,

00:40:53.516 --> 00:40:56.670
which should put it right there.

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:59.460
Let's see if we can do
that, oh, and there it is.

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:02.370
So we can actually jump
back and forth between code,

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:04.470
and the drag and drop interface.

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:09.470
So it's not about dumbing
something down, not at all.

00:41:10.133 --> 00:41:13.590
With QJS, you have access
to this quantum circuit

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:17.040
both through code and the
drag and drop interface,

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.672
and you can fluidly switch back and forth

00:41:19.672 --> 00:41:24.672
to whatever has least cognitive
overhead in that moment.

00:41:25.296 --> 00:41:28.410
Let's say I need to get
rid of everything here

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:29.880
in moment two.

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:31.500
I could certainly code that,

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:34.470
or I could just select
it and throw it away.

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:39.470
So I just want to emphasize
that designing a user interface

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.627
and offering pictures
to look at and click on

00:41:42.627 --> 00:41:45.450
is never about dumbing something down.

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:48.463
It's about making tasks
easier and more efficient

00:41:48.463 --> 00:41:51.603
for us human beings that require that.

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:56.520
So QJS definitely sits
on the software side.

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:58.920
But I happened to work
at Amazon for a while,

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:00.720
and Amazon just so happened to be working

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:04.050
on a quantum service called Amazon Braket.

00:42:04.050 --> 00:42:07.080
You can access Braket through
a prepared Jupyter notebook,

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:09.210
if you know what that means,

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:11.642
which is similar to
other quantum services,

00:42:11.642 --> 00:42:13.293
quantum cloud services.

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:16.860
So anyway, here's a little
demo video that I made

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:19.950
just a few months before
Braket launched publicly,

00:42:19.950 --> 00:42:22.293
using the QJS interface to drive it.

00:42:23.656 --> 00:42:25.920
So here I have a Jupyter notebook

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:28.614
with Amazon Braket loaded,

00:42:28.614 --> 00:42:32.430
which means that I can use Python code.

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:34.890
Braket is a Python library.

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:38.530
And I can use that to
build quantum circuits

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:41.915
that I can then run on a
simulator in the cloud,

00:42:41.915 --> 00:42:45.120
or I can run on quantum hardware,

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:48.120
because Amazon has
partnered with different

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:49.893
quantum hardware vendors.

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:55.826
And so we can do that, we can
offer that to our customers,

00:42:55.826 --> 00:42:58.350
and that's pretty exciting.

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:00.183
But what's also exciting is this.

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:06.110
With just a few lines of HTML,
really, two lines of HTML,

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:11.660
I can inject QJS into
this Jupyter notebook,

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:14.280
it's now injected.

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:19.280
And now I can inject my
quantum circuit composer.

00:43:20.666 --> 00:43:24.449
And this composer, which
should look awfully familiar,

00:43:24.449 --> 00:43:27.570
is writing Braket code.

00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:30.732
So this is actually Braket Python code,

00:43:30.732 --> 00:43:33.660
which right now is set to run
on the simulator in the cloud

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:35.913
so that we can get quicker results.

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:37.710
But if you wanted to,

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:42.450
you could switch what
device we're running on,

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:45.720
and you could actually run
this on quantum hardware,

00:43:45.720 --> 00:43:48.240
and get true quantum results.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:49.233
So check this out.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:53.847
Down here, you can see where
it says our QJS circuit

00:43:53.847 --> 00:43:56.040
equals a new circuit,

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:59.340
and it has a placeholder identity gate.

00:43:59.340 --> 00:44:02.700
This is Braket code, and we're
about to watch it change,

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:05.013
as I start to compose stuff.

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:15.660
Look at that, look how
it put a Hadamard gate,

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:18.270
and created a C-NOT gate.

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:21.090
And we can take that and we can run that.

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:23.493
What this is is a coin flip circuit.

00:44:24.563 --> 00:44:26.190
And we're waiting for the output

00:44:26.190 --> 00:44:27.590
to come back from the cloud.

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:33.650
So Hadamard puts the qubit
into a state of superposition,

00:44:34.151 --> 00:44:39.151
and then this distributes
that superposition

00:44:40.080 --> 00:44:44.670
to the qubit on the second register here.

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:49.527
And so our two possible
outcomes are 00 and 11,

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:52.800
and you can see, we've
gotten a result back

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:54.060
from the simulator.

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:56.520
It's run that simulation a hundred times,

00:44:56.520 --> 00:45:00.129
which is a customizable number.

00:45:00.129 --> 00:45:02.550
And it's come back roughly even.

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:07.503
54 times it came out 00,
and 46 times it came out 11.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:10.473
So here's the deal.

00:45:11.365 --> 00:45:15.660
We can drag and drop and
build circuits as we please

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.510
using the QJS drag and drop interface.

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:21.429
We can even edit that thing
and get real time reports

00:45:21.429 --> 00:45:24.540
in the JavaScript console still.

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:27.300
And it is building Amazon Braket code,

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:29.820
which we can run on a simulator,

00:45:29.820 --> 00:45:33.000
or on actual quantum hardware.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:34.923
So that is pretty cool.

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:39.210
- So that's it for Quantum JavaScript.

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:40.890
- [Audience Member] I have a question.

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:41.723
- Yeah.

00:45:41.723 --> 00:45:42.870
- [Audience Member] For the Amazon Braket,

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:45.060
is it a hardware quantum computer,

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:47.117
or it's also simulated?

00:45:47.117 --> 00:45:47.950
- Both.

00:45:47.950 --> 00:45:49.170
So they have their own,

00:45:49.170 --> 00:45:50.730
because they have a huge
cloud infrastructure,

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:52.530
they have a pretty efficient simulator

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:53.850
that runs in the cloud.

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:56.310
But then they partner with Rigetti,

00:45:56.310 --> 00:46:00.090
and, oh gosh, now the
caffeine's wearing off.

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:02.280
but they have a few
different hardware partners,

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:05.280
and so you can actually select
which system you want to use.

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:12.213
All right. Maybe it's
time to wrap this up.

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:16.380
And that's me, and the
sorts of things that I make.

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:18.360
I'm happy to take more questions,

00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:20.130
or if you're curious about other work,

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:21.813
I'm happy to show more things.

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:24.393
But we can open it up a bit.

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:26.153
- [Florian] Why do we focus

00:46:26.153 --> 00:46:28.653
on the web based application?

00:46:29.861 --> 00:46:31.860
- Well, I guess there are two reasons

00:46:31.860 --> 00:46:34.320
that I focus on web based stuff.

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:37.833
One is that's where I learned
to code a lot of things.

00:46:38.730 --> 00:46:42.390
Because it's, you have
access to the source code,

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:44.730
even if it's not technically open source,

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:46.767
everything that's happening
on the front end is there,

00:46:46.767 --> 00:46:49.020
you can just view source in the browser,

00:46:49.020 --> 00:46:50.430
and start to pull it apart.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:51.360
- [Audience Member] You can?

00:46:51.360 --> 00:46:52.680
- Yes, yeah.

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:54.600
Things are a little more
locked down these days,

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:59.127
but there's sort of the wild
west days of the late '90s

00:46:59.127 --> 00:47:04.127
and early 2000s where you
could take apart websites

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:05.913
to a much greater degree.

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:09.120
So that's how I learned
to do a lot of stuff.

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:12.900
The other reason is it's
so immediately sharable.

00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:15.870
And I think that's pretty highly valued,

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:17.850
and everyone has a web
browser on their computer,

00:47:17.850 --> 00:47:21.420
so you can also be sure
that you can run things.

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:26.420
And yeah, it's just a huge,
I wanna say market share,

00:47:26.949 --> 00:47:28.623
and that's the wrong word for it.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:31.410
It's a huge distribution channel.

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:34.372
- [Florian] Is there any limitation?

00:47:34.372 --> 00:47:36.270
Can you do everything you
want with the browser?

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:37.920
Do you have sometimes, like what's the-

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:40.053
- Oh, there are definitely limitations.

00:47:41.040 --> 00:47:43.350
But a lot of them are
melting away every day.

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:45.690
So for instance, right
now I'm playing with

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:50.690
a hardware accelerated
simulation algorithm for QJS.

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:56.050
Because you have, through the web GPU API,

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:59.610
you can get right onto
the graphics card hardware

00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:01.980
and use the hardware
acceleration for that.

00:48:01.980 --> 00:48:05.046
And in this case, instead
of making fancy graphics,

00:48:05.046 --> 00:48:09.573
I'm just multiplying the large
matrices of quantum states.

00:48:14.776 --> 00:48:15.609
(laughs)

00:48:15.609 --> 00:48:16.620
- [Audience Member] How
did you get interested

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:18.698
in quantum computing?

00:48:18.698 --> 00:48:21.960
- Oh, well, I should probably have

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:24.690
a real conceptual answer for
this, but just for a long time,

00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:26.640
I thought quantum physics
and quantum mechanics

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:27.963
sounded really cool.

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:30.600
I was like, wow, that's neat.

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.540
And I kept trying to wrap my
head around it and I couldn't.

00:48:33.540 --> 00:48:37.140
But I keep watching the
pop science explanations

00:48:37.140 --> 00:48:39.060
of what quantum is, which,

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:40.980
once you start learning anything about it,

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:42.940
you're like, that's stupid.

00:48:42.940 --> 00:48:47.370
But then I came across a video
by this guy, Andrew Heller,

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:49.650
I think is his name, from Microsoft.

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:51.990
And he gave an internal Microsoft lecture

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:53.257
that they published called

00:48:53.257 --> 00:48:56.040
"Quantum Computing for
Computer Scientists",

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:59.763
which is him summarizing his
favorite book on the subject.

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:04.980
And suddenly the way he was
describing it just as coding

00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:05.910
made a lot of sense.

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:08.790
And I thought, oh my god, I
bet I could just write that.

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:10.920
And I bet I could write
it for the browser,

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:12.780
and then start sharing it with folks.

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:17.250
And that was my first actual understanding

00:49:17.250 --> 00:49:19.350
of how some quantum stuff works.

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:22.500
I would say I still don't
understand any of it.

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:25.713
But I could build a functioning simulator.

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:32.515
Any other questions?

00:49:32.515 --> 00:49:34.353
- Yeah.

00:49:34.353 --> 00:49:35.220
- [Audience Member] Just
a kind of follow up.

00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:39.047
So when would someone use
the web based application

00:49:39.047 --> 00:49:39.880
or to a desktop based application?

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.280
- That's a good question,
but I'm not sure.

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.080
There must be desktop applications,

00:49:49.080 --> 00:49:52.350
but when I think about Qiskit,

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:56.220
or just the other things that I've seen

00:49:56.220 --> 00:49:58.200
that are basically running
in Jupyter notebooks,

00:49:58.200 --> 00:50:00.330
those things are all web based.

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:02.250
Even if they're presenting themselves

00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:04.020
as a native application, under the hood,

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:08.280
they're basically running
little web servers and the like.

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:10.530
And it seems like that's okay,

00:50:10.530 --> 00:50:12.963
because what you really want to do,

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:15.870
is you just want to have
an understandable interface

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:18.690
to take your thoughts
and put them in a way

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:20.340
the computer can understand.

00:50:20.340 --> 00:50:21.330
And then you're gonna push that

00:50:21.330 --> 00:50:23.040
to real quantum hardware anyway.

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:27.690
So the medium in between
doesn't really matter as much.

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:29.220
And even with the simulation stuff,

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:31.200
either you're simulating on the cloud,

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:33.060
so it's just sending the instructions

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:35.299
to many computers to come back.

00:50:35.299 --> 00:50:37.740
Or you're trying to do
hardware acceleration

00:50:37.740 --> 00:50:39.090
on your own local machine.

00:50:39.090 --> 00:50:41.640
And you can do all of
that through a browser.

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:44.140
So you don't necessarily
need to worry about that.

00:50:45.733 --> 00:50:47.340
- Okay.

00:50:47.340 --> 00:50:51.360
Thank you very much for
coming, and for your questions.

00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:53.910
Let's thank Stewart Smith one more time,

00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:55.683
and then we'll see you, all of us,

00:50:55.683 --> 00:50:58.554
for the public event in the fall.

00:50:58.554 --> 00:50:59.951
Thank you very much!

00:50:59.951 --> 00:51:01.523
(audience applauding)

